---
import Article from '../../components/Article.astro';

const title = 'FreeCell Strategy: How to Win (Almost) Every Deal | CardHearth';
const description =
  'Learn a reliable FreeCell strategy. Every card is dealt face-up, so winning is about planning: protect your free cells, open an empty column, read sequences ahead, and dig out buried aces.';
const updated = '2026-06-13';
const heading = 'FreeCell Strategy: How to Win (Almost) Every Deal';
const subtitle =
  'FreeCell hides nothing — almost every deal is solvable. The skill is planning, and these principles teach you how.';

const breadcrumb = [
  { name: 'CardHearth', href: '/' },
  { name: 'Guides', href: '/guides/' },
  { name: 'FreeCell Strategy', href: '/guides/freecell-strategy/' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Are all FreeCell games winnable?',
    a: 'Almost. Of the classic 32,000 numbered Microsoft deals, only one — game #11982 — has been proven unsolvable; every other deal can be won with correct play. Because nothing is hidden, a game you lose is almost always a solvable game you misplayed rather than bad luck. That is what makes FreeCell so rewarding to study.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How many cards can I move at once?',
    a: 'You can only move one card at a time, but free cells and empty columns let you shuffle a run as a group. The maximum is (1 + number of empty free cells) × 2^(number of empty tableau columns). So with all four free cells open and no empty columns you can move five cards; open one column and that doubles to ten. Empty cells and columns are your mobility — guard them.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is FreeCell harder than Klondike?',
    a: 'In one sense it is far easier: nearly every FreeCell deal is winnable, while many Klondike deals simply cannot be won. But FreeCell rewards careful thought over luck. There is no stock to draw and nothing hidden, so a loss is on you — which makes it the better game for learning deliberate, several-moves-ahead planning.',
  },
];

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'FreeCell Strategy: How to Win (Almost) Every Deal',
    description,
    datePublished: updated,
    dateModified: updated,
    author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Jared', url: 'https://cardhearth.com/about/' },
    publisher: {
      '@type': 'Organization',
      name: 'CardHearth',
      url: 'https://cardhearth.com/',
      logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: 'https://cardhearth.com/logo.png' },
    },
    mainEntityOfPage: 'https://cardhearth.com/guides/freecell-strategy/',
    image: 'https://cardhearth.com/og/freecell.png',
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: breadcrumb.map((c, i) => ({
      '@type': 'ListItem',
      position: i + 1,
      name: c.name,
      item: `https://cardhearth.com${c.href}`,
    })),
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: f.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
    })),
  },
];
---

<Article
  title={title}
  description={description}
  heading={heading}
  subtitle={subtitle}
  updated={updated}
  schema={schema}
  breadcrumb={breadcrumb}
  cta={{ href: '/freecell/', label: 'Play FreeCell' }}
>
  <p>
    FreeCell is the thinking player's solitaire. Unlike Klondike, <strong>every card is dealt
    face-up from the start</strong>, so there is no hidden information and no luck of the draw —
    the entire game sits in front of you. That single difference changes everything: winning is
    not about hoping the next card helps, it's about <em>planning</em>. And the planning pays off,
    because almost every deal can be won. This guide explains the principles that turn a visible
    board into a solved one, and <em>why</em> each one matters.
  </p>

  <h2>The goal, in one sentence</h2>
  <p>
    Build the four <strong>foundations</strong> upward by suit, Ace to King. You do that by
    arranging the eight <strong>tableau</strong> columns into descending runs of alternating
    colour (a red Eight on a black Nine), using the four <strong>free cells</strong> — each of
    which holds exactly one card — as temporary storage. If the rules are new, the quickest way to
    learn is to <a href="/freecell/">play a hand</a> with hints on.
  </p>

  <h2>The one mechanic that decides everything</h2>
  <p>
    You can only ever move <strong>one card at a time</strong>. The reason you appear to move whole
    runs is that free cells and empty columns let the game shuffle the cards for you. The maximum
    number you can shift in a single "supermove" is:
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>(1 + empty free cells) × 2<sup>(empty tableau columns)</sup></strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    With four free cells open and no empty columns, that's five cards. Free up a single column and
    it doubles to ten. In other words, <strong>your empty free cells and empty columns are your
    mobility</strong> — they are the resource the whole game is fought over. Guarding them, and
    creating more of them, is the heart of FreeCell strategy.
  </p>

  <h2>Five principles that win games</h2>

  <h3>1. Keep your free cells empty</h3>
  <p>
    Treat parking a card in a free cell as <strong>borrowing</strong> — a debt you must have a
    plan to repay. Every cell you fill cuts your supermove size and your options. Four occupied
    cells with no way to unload them is the single most common way to lose a winnable deal. Use a
    cell only when you can see how the card comes back out.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Work to empty a column early</h3>
  <p>
    An empty column is worth far more than an empty free cell, because it <em>multiplies</em> how
    much you can move rather than just adding one. Emptying even one column early transforms the
    board, so prioritise consolidating short piles to open a space. Just don't fill a hard-won
    empty column with a stray card unless it advances your plan.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Plan several moves ahead before committing</h3>
  <p>
    Because the whole deal is visible, you can — and should — read sequences out in your head
    before touching anything. Trace where a card will go, what it frees, and how you'll recover
    any cells you spend, all the way to the foundation. The best FreeCell players move slowly and
    deliberately; the board isn't going anywhere.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Send low cards up — but not the ones you still need</h3>
  <p>
    Getting Aces and Twos to the foundations frees space and unclogs the tableau. But don't strip
    the board of cards you still need to <em>receive</em> runs. A Five you bank too early might be
    exactly the landing spot a black Four and red Three were waiting for. Promote a card when it
    no longer has work to do on the tableau, not the instant it becomes legal.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Hunt for buried aces and twos — and plan the dig</h3>
  <p>
    Aces and Twos stuck near the bottom of a long column block everything above their suit. Spot
    them at the very start and plan the excavation: which cells and empty columns you'll spend to
    reach them, and how you'll recover afterwards. Digging out a deep Ace early often unlocks the
    entire deal.
  </p>

  <h2>Why CardHearth is a great place to learn</h2>
  <p>
    FreeCell rewards deliberate practice, and CardHearth is built for it: <strong>unlimited
    undo</strong> lets you explore a line and take it back if it dead-ends, and <strong>hints</strong>
    nudge you when you're genuinely stuck. Every deal is <strong>numbered and shareable</strong>,
    so you can replay the exact same hand to test a better plan — or hand a friend a tough deal and
    see who solves it first.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes that lose games</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Filling all four free cells with no exit plan</strong> — the number-one win-killer.</li>
    <li><strong>Burying an ace</strong> under a long column instead of digging it out early.</li>
    <li><strong>Sending cards to the foundation too eagerly</strong> when they were still needed on
      the tableau.</li>
    <li><strong>Never creating an empty column</strong>, leaving your supermoves tiny all game.</li>
    <li><strong>Moving on autopilot</strong> — with everything visible, a few seconds of planning
      beats a dozen reflex moves.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  {
    faq.map((f) => (
      <details class="faq">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold">{f.q}</summary>
        <p class="mt-2 opacity-90">{f.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))
  }

  <h2>Keep going</h2>
  <p>
    Ready to put it into practice? <a href="/freecell/">Play FreeCell</a> now, or branch out:
    <a href="/guides/how-to-win-klondike-solitaire/">how to win Klondike Solitaire</a> and
    <a href="/guides/spider-solitaire-strategy/">Spider Solitaire strategy</a> reward a different
    kind of planning, and our <a href="/guides/">guides hub</a> covers the whole CardHearth library.
  </p>
</Article>
